<div id="p3" class="ltx_para ltx_align_center">
<p id="p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:144%;">Supplementary Material 
<br class="ltx_break"></span></p>
</div>
<div id="id2" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_center">
    
<div id="p4" class="ltx_para"><img src="2603.29034v1/viewdataset2.png" id="g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="width:49.083em; height:17.666em;aspect-ratio:815/294;" width="815" height="294" alt="[Uncaptioned image]">
</div>
    
<figure id="Sx1.F11" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_center">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:144%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="Sx1.F11.11" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:63%;">Figure 11</span>: </span><span id="Sx1.F11.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:63%;">Example images used <span id="Sx1.F11.12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium ltx_font_smallcaps">Snp</span> training dataset<span id="Sx1.F11.12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">(a) Structured noise images, from dead leaves and statistical image generation models , from Looking at Noise <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. (b) Unstructured noise images, Uniform and gaussian noise.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
  
</div>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Structured and Unstructured noises used for pretraining</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#Sx1.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ The Surprising Effectiveness of Noise Pretraining for Implicit Neural Representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> showcase example images used for <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Snp</span> pretraining for both structured (from Baradad et.al <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>) and unstructured noises (uniform and Gaussian). For more details on how structured noise images are generated, we refer the reader to Baradad et.al <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span><span id="S6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Snp</span> pretrain and fitting procedures</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first provide the reader with an overview of conventional INRs followed by detailed training and fitting procedures for <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Snp</span> INRs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conventional INRs.</span> Typically an INR, denoted as <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> with parameters <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> learns a mapping between <math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> dimensional input coordinates, <math id="S6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> dimensional signal, <math id="S6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}\in\mathcal{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}\in\mathcal{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. INR parameters <math id="S6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> are iteratively optimized by minimizing an objective function, such as mean squared error, to yield the optimal learned signal representation <math id="S6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<table id="S6.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\theta^{*}=\arg\min_{\theta}\mid\mid f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x})-\mathbf{y}(\mathbf{x})\mid\mid^{2}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>θ</mi></munder><mo lspace="0em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo rspace="0.0835em">∣</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∣</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.0835em">∣</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\theta^{*}=\arg\min_{\theta}\mid\mid f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x})-\mathbf{y}(\mathbf{x})\mid\mid^{2}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Snp INRs.</span> <span id="S6.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Snp</span> follows the design of the <span id="S6.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Strainer</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> INR framework. As described in <span id="S6.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Strainer</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>, at pretraining time, <span id="S6.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Snp</span> shares initial <math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L-1</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S6.p3.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">encoder</em> layers while jointly fitting <math id="S6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> signals, each with their respective signal specific decoder. At test-time, <span id="S6.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Snp</span> retains the pretrained encoder weights as an initialization along with randomly initialized decoder and proceeds to fit an unseen signal.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We define the initial encoder layers of <span id="S6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Snp</span> as <math id="S6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>. During pretraining stage, for each train signal <math id="S6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define signal specific decoders denoted by <math id="S6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\psi}^{i}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\psi}^{i}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>. We define <span id="S6.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Snp</span> INR <math id="S6.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\Theta}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\Theta}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> below in Eq. <a href="#S6.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 6 Snp pretrain and fitting procedures ‣ The Surprising Effectiveness of Noise Pretraining for Implicit Neural Representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S6.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle h_{\Theta}(\mathbf{x})=g^{i}_{\psi}\circ f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle h_{\Theta}(\mathbf{x})=g^{i}_{\psi}\circ f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p6.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math> is the collective set of encoder weights <math id="S6.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> and decoder weights <math id="S6.p6.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\psi^{1}\cdots\psi^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mi>ψ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi^{1}\cdots\psi^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p7.1" class="ltx_p">During pretraining, we fit <math id="S6.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> signals of the same noise-type jointly to <span id="S6.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Snp</span> for <math id="S6.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> steps, following Eq <a href="#S6.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 6 Snp pretrain and fitting procedures ‣ The Surprising Effectiveness of Noise Pretraining for Implicit Neural Representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, to obtain learned encoder initialization <math id="S6.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p8" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S6.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Theta^{*}=\arg\min_{\Theta}\sum_{i=1}^{i=N}\mid\mid g_{\psi}^{i}\circ f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x})-\mathbf{y^{i}}(\mathbf{x})\mid\mid^{2}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mmultiscripts><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></munderover><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mstyle><mo lspace="0em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo rspace="0.0835em">∣</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>𝐢</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∣</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.0835em">∣</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Theta^{*}=\arg\min_{\Theta}\sum_{i=1}^{i=N}\mid\mid g_{\psi}^{i}\circ f_{\theta}(\mathbf{x})-\mathbf{y^{i}}(\mathbf{x})\mid\mid^{2}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p9.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.p9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Theta^{*}=[\theta^{*},\psi^{1*},\cdots,\psi^{N*}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta^{*}=[\theta^{*},\psi^{1*},\cdots,\psi^{N*}]</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p10.1" class="ltx_p">At test-time, <span id="S6.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Snp</span> is initialized learned encoder weights <math id="S6.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta=\theta^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta=\theta^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> along with a randomly initialized decoder <math id="S6.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, we fit the test-signal for <math id="S6.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> steps to the pretrained <span id="S6.p10.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Snp</span> INR as shown in Eq. <a href="#S6.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ 6 Snp pretrain and fitting procedures ‣ The Surprising Effectiveness of Noise Pretraining for Implicit Neural Representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p11" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S6.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle[\theta^{*},\psi^{*}]\leftarrow\arg\min_{\theta,\psi}\mid\mid g_{\psi}\circ f_{\theta=\theta^{*}}(\mathbf{x})-\mathbf{y}(\mathbf{x})\mid\mid^{2}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ψ</mi></mrow></munder></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0em">|</mo><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∣</mo></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0.0835em">∣</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle[\theta^{*},\psi^{*}]\leftarrow\arg\min_{\theta,\psi}\mid\mid g_{\psi}\circ f_{\theta=\theta^{*}}(\mathbf{x})-\mathbf{y}(\mathbf{x})\mid\mid^{2}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Training specifications</h2>

<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.1 </span>Image and Video fitting</h3>

<div id="S7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Image fitting.</span> We describe the pretraining and fitting procedure for <span id="S7.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Snp</span> INRs. For pretraining, we follow the process outlined in <span id="S7.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Strainer</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> and use <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=10</annotation></semantics></math> images (which has previously shown success in learning high quality initialization in <span id="S7.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Strainer</span>) for each type of noise (illustrated in Fig. <a href="#Sx1.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ The Surprising Effectiveness of Noise Pretraining for Implicit Neural Representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>). At test-time, we retain the pretrained features as initialization of the <em id="S7.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">encoder</em> or first <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L-1</annotation></semantics></math> layers and randomly initialize the last <em id="S7.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">decoder</em> layer. We fit each test image for <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2000</annotation></semantics></math> iterations using Adam optimizer with learning rate of <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>. All hidden layers are of width <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>256</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Video fitting.</span> We adopt ResField as our video-fitting backbone to apply the <span id="S7.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Snp</span> initialization. ResField’s parameters are decomposed into temporally shared weight and per-frame low-rank residuals. We transfer the weights learned during <span id="S7.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Snp</span> noise pre-training to the shared branch, while initializing the residual factors to zero <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, i.e., <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2}),\,B=0,\,\Delta W=BA=0\text{ at the first iteration}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> at the first iteration</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2}),\,B=0,\,\Delta W=BA=0\text{ at the first iteration}</annotation></semantics></math>. We employ an MLP with four hidden layers of width 256 for the shared weights and rank-10 residual weights.
Each video is optimized for <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>5</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{5}</annotation></semantics></math> iterations using the Adam optimizer
(learning rate <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>) and a cosine-annealing learning-rate schedule. We perform pre-training on noise images rather than noise videos, and then successfully transfer the resulting model weights from the image domain to the video domain. This strategy is highly practical, as it substantially reduces both pre-training time and the complexity of pretraining data generation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Image and Video Denoising.</span> For image and video denoising, we simulate a Poisson process to add synthetic noise of photon count of 30, resulting in a read-out noise of 2 dB to each image/video frame. At test-time we initialize the <span id="S7.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Snp</span> encoder using the corresponding weights from fitting tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We use the Alpine INR framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> for pretraining and test-time signal fitting for <span id="S7.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Snp</span> INRs.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>More results on image and video fitting</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.3 Image and Video Fitting Results ‣ 3 Experimental Analysis ‣ The Surprising Effectiveness of Noise Pretraining for Implicit Neural Representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> (in main text) presented a brief snapshot of our experimental evaluation. We provide a more detailed analysis in Tables <a href="#S8.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 8 More results on image and video fitting ‣ The Surprising Effectiveness of Noise Pretraining for Implicit Neural Representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, <a href="#S8.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 8 More results on image and video fitting ‣ The Surprising Effectiveness of Noise Pretraining for Implicit Neural Representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, and Table <a href="#S8.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ 8 More results on image and video fitting ‣ The Surprising Effectiveness of Noise Pretraining for Implicit Neural Representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> and report mean values of PSNR, SSIM, and LPIPS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite> and 1 std dev. for randomly initialized <span id="S8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Siren</span>, data-driven baselines such as <span id="S8.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Strainer</span>, and <span id="S8.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Snp</span> variants for image fitting on CelebA-HQ,AFHQ, and OASIS-MRI datasets. We observe that <span id="S8.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Snp</span> pretrained on uniform and Gaussian noise strikingly, achieves a PSNR of <math id="S8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="80db+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>80</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">80db+</annotation></semantics></math>, outperforming all other methods. On structured noise initializations, <span id="S8.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Snp</span>: Spectrum performs best, almost matching <span id="S8.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Strainer</span>. Figures <a href="#S8.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ 8 More results on image and video fitting ‣ The Surprising Effectiveness of Noise Pretraining for Implicit Neural Representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> and  <a href="#S8.F12" title="Figure 12 ‣ 8 More results on image and video fitting ‣ The Surprising Effectiveness of Noise Pretraining for Implicit Neural Representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> also show image fitting trajectories for AFHQ and OASIS-MRI datasets respectively and we observe that <span id="S8.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Snp</span> pretrained on all noise types to quickly converge to high quality signal representations. Notably, <span id="S8.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Snp</span>: Uniform achieves <span id="S8.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Siren</span> level performance <math id="S8.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="20\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20\times</annotation></semantics></math> faster and <span id="S8.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Strainer</span> level performance <math id="S8.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="4\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\times</annotation></semantics></math> faster when evaluated on CelebA-HQ ( see Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.3 Image and Video Fitting Results ‣ 3 Experimental Analysis ‣ The Surprising Effectiveness of Noise Pretraining for Implicit Neural Representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> in main text) and on AFHQ and OASIS-MRI datasets as shown in Fig. <a href="#S8.F12" title="Figure 12 ‣ 8 More results on image and video fitting ‣ The Surprising Effectiveness of Noise Pretraining for Implicit Neural Representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> and Fig. <a href="#S8.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ 8 More results on image and video fitting ‣ The Surprising Effectiveness of Noise Pretraining for Implicit Neural Representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> in attached supplement.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S8.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S8.T4.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 4</span>: </span><span id="S8.T4.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Image fitting results on CelebA-HQ.<span id="S8.T4.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> We present mean PSNR(in db), SSIM and LPIPS with 1 std.dev. for all test methods at <math id="S8.T4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=2000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=2000</annotation></semantics></math> iterations, from vanilla randomly initialized (<span id="S8.T4.8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Siren</span>), to data driven methods such as Metalearned <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, <span id="S8.T4.8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Strainer</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>, and <span id="S8.T4.8.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Snp</span> variants trained on structured and unstructured noises.</span></span></figcaption>
<div id="S8.T4.9" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:267.5pt;height:128pt;vertical-align:-62.4pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-57.3pt,27.4pt) scale(0.7,0.7) ;">
<table id="S8.T4.9.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
